image: lessons/bible/saved-christian-now-what-title.jpg | So you’re saved… Now What? A Beginner’s Guide to Christian Living | KJVO Sunday School Resources · Fundamental Foundations Training

=== I. The Gospel Confirmed
--- Session One: the gospel confirmed
kicker: Session one
lead: Before we talk about growing, let’s make sure the ground is solid. You have already believed the gospel and been saved. This session is not to make you doubt that, but to root it so deep that no one, including the devil himself, can shake it loose. We begin where the Bible begins the good news: God’s love, man’s need, and Christ’s finished work.

--- The Gospel in a verse
scripture: John 3:16-18
p: Notice the shape of it: God’s love is the source, Christ’s gift is the means, and belief, simple trust in Him, is the only response required. Not belief plus baptism, not belief plus church membership, not belief plus a certain feeling. Belief alone, in Him alone.

--- The Romans Road
p: Paul’s letter to the Romans lays the same truth out step by step. Walk it slowly. This is the road you walked to get saved, and it is the road you will walk with others.
fig: All have sinned | Romans 3:23
fig: The wages of sin | Romans 6:23
fig: Christ died for us | Romans 5:8
fig: Confess and believe | Romans 10:9-10; Romans 10:13

--- You can know you have eternal life
scripture: 1 John 5:10-13
callout: **Teaching point.** John didn’t write “that ye may hope” or “that ye may wish”; he wrote *that ye may know*. Assurance isn’t pride; it’s simply taking God at His word. If He said it, and you believed it, it’s settled.

--- Eternal security: once saved, kept
p: If salvation depended on you keeping yourself saved, none of us would make it. But salvation was never a transaction you maintain; it’s a gift God keeps. Nothing in all creation is strong enough to undo what Christ has done.
scripture: Romans 8:35-39
p: Everything that follows in this course (baptism, prayer, Bible study, godly living) is not how you *stay* saved. It’s how a saved person grows in faith and grace.

=== II. Baptism, an outward sign
--- Baptism: an outward sign
kicker: Session two
lead: Baptism saves no one. You were already saved the moment you believed. But the very first thing the early church did with a new believer was baptize him. It’s not a suggestion for the “serious” Christian later on; it’s the normal, immediate next step for every believer. Think of it as a public wedding after a private engagement: the commitment was already real, but now everyone gets to see it. Or think of a wedding ring: that ring is the outward sign, but without it you are still married. Baptism works the same way: it doesn’t create the marriage between you and Christ, it displays one that already exists.

--- Two reasons to be baptized
panel: An outward sign to others | It tells the world, without a word, that you have died to your old life and been raised to a new one (buried under the water, raised up out of it).
panel: An act of obedience to God | Christ commanded it, and a saved heart wants to obey, not to earn anything, but because it loves the One who saved it.

--- What the picture means
p: Why go under the water at all? Because baptism isn’t just a ritual. It’s a picture Paul explains in plain terms: going down into the water pictures Christ’s death and burial, and coming back up pictures His resurrection. You are acting out the gospel with your own body.
scripture: Romans 6:3-5 | mark: buried with him; in the likeness

--- A new believer gets baptized: the Ethiopian
p: Acts 8 gives us the clearest picture in Scripture of what happens the moment a new convert understands the gospel. Read it as one flowing account.
scripture: Acts 8:26-35

--- What doth hinder me to be baptized?
scripture: Acts 8:36-39 | mark: what doth hinder me to be baptized?; If thou believest with all thine heart, thou mayest.
p: Notice what the eunuch did the moment he understood: he didn’t wait for a “right time,” a special service, or years of preparation. There was water. He believed. He was baptized. He went on his way rejoicing. That’s the pattern for you too.

--- Going on toward maturity
p: Baptism is a foundation stone, not the finish line. The writer of Hebrews names it as one of several “first principles” a believer lays down once, and then builds on for the rest of life. The next five sessions of this course are that building.
scripture: Hebrews 6:1-3

=== III. Every believer a witness
--- Every believer a witness
kicker: Session three
big: Proverbs 11:30
p: Here’s good news you may not have heard yet: you don’t need years of Bible school to point someone to Christ. You just need to have met Him. Scripture’s clearest illustration of this is a woman who had known Jesus for less than an hour before she was leading her whole town to Him.

--- The woman at the well
p: *John 4:4-42, in brief:* Jesus, tired from His journey, stops at a well in Samaria at noon and asks a Samaritan woman for a drink, a request that broke two social barriers at once, since Jews had no dealings with Samaritans, and a rabbi did not speak to a woman alone in public. He offers her “living water,” and when she asks for it, He gently exposes the real thirst in her life: five failed marriages and a man she isn’t married to now. Rather than running from that, she runs toward Him, perceiving Him as a prophet, then questioning Him about worship, and finally hearing Him say plainly, “I that speak unto thee am he,” the Messiah Himself.
refs: John 4:4-42

--- She does not wait
p: She does not go get trained. She does not wait until she has her own life sorted out. She leaves her water pot standing right there and goes straight into the city, telling everyone, “Come, see a man, which told me all things that ever I did: is not this the Christ?” And John tells us many of the Samaritans believed on Him *because of the woman’s testimony*, before they ever met Jesus themselves. A woman with a broken past and no theological training became the first missionary to Samaria, simply by telling what she knew.
callout: **Teaching point.** You already know enough to win someone to Christ. You know that you were lost, that Jesus saved you, and how. That is a testimony no one can argue with, and it’s exactly what the woman at the well had.

--- The Great Commission
scripture: Matthew 28:18-20
lit: Witnesses unto the uttermost part | Acts 1:8
p: This wasn’t given only to preachers. It was given to every believer in the room (fishermen, tax collectors, ordinary people) and it still stands today. Soul-winning is not a specialty; it’s the normal Christian life.

--- Living so your witness isn’t undone
p: What you say about Christ matters. So does how you live in front of the people watching you say it.
fig: Chaste conversation | 1 Peter 3:1-2
fig: Let your light so shine | Matthew 5:14-16
fig: Abstain from all appearance of evil | 1 Thessalonians 5:22

--- Conversation means conduct
callout: “Chaste conversation” here doesn’t just mean clean speech. *Conversation* in the King James English means your whole conduct, your manner of life. A watching world reads that conduct like a letter, long before it ever reads a Bible verse. Guard it carefully; a single careless season can silence years of testimony.

=== IV. Prayer, talking with the Father
--- Prayer: talking with the Father
kicker: Session four
lead: Prayer is simply talking to God as your Father, because that’s exactly what He’s become to you. Not a formula to unlock blessings, not a performance for others to hear, but a relationship. Paul tells believers plainly to *pray without ceasing* ([[1 Thessalonians 5:17]]): not one long unbroken prayer all day, but a heart that stays in conversation with God through everything the day brings.

--- The model prayer
p: When His disciples asked Jesus to teach them to pray, He didn’t hand them a theory. He gave them a pattern.
scripture: Matthew 6:9-13

--- The ACTS pattern
p: Bible teachers have long noticed that the Lord’s Prayer naturally breaks into four movements, an easy pattern to carry into your own prayer time, remembered by the word ACTS.
image: lessons/bible/saved-christian-now-what-acts.jpg | Adult guide to the ACTS prayer model: Adoration, Confession, Thanksgiving, Supplication | ADORATION · CONFESSION · THANKSGIVING · SUPPLICATION

--- Psalms for the pattern: A and C
p: The Psalms give us some of the best examples in Scripture of each part of that pattern. Read each one slowly. Better yet, pray it back to God in your own words.
fig: A, Adoration (praising God for who He is) | Psalm 8:1; Psalm 145:3
fig: C, Confession (agreeing with God about sin) | Psalm 51:1-3; Psalm 32:5

--- Psalms for the pattern: T and S
fig: T, Thanksgiving (gratitude for what He has done) | Psalm 100:4; Psalm 103:1-2
fig: S, Supplication (asking for what we need) | Psalm 86:1-2; Psalm 143:10

--- When you don’t know how to pray
scripture: Romans 8:26-27
callout: **Teaching point.** Yes, you can pray amiss. [[James 4:3]] warns that asking with wrong motives, purely to spend it on yourself, gets you nowhere. But don’t let that make you afraid to pray. The Spirit corrects and carries your prayer even when your words fall short, and God **answers prayer with what you actually need**, not always what you asked for.

--- Asking amiss, and a Father who gives
fig: Asking amiss | James 4:3
fig: God shall supply all your need | Philippians 4:19
fig: A Father who gives good gifts | Matthew 7:9-11
fig: The confidence we have in prayer | 1 John 5:14-15

=== V. Bible study, feeding on the word
--- Bible study: feeding on the word
kicker: Session five
lead: Prayer is you talking to God. Bible study is God talking to you. A believer who prays but never opens the Word ends up with a lot of opinions and very little truth to test them against. You need both.
big: 2 Timothy 2:15-16 | mark: Study; rightly dividing the word of truth
p: “Study” here isn’t a suggestion to skim. It means to work at it, the way a tradesman works at his craft until he no longer has to guess. You are not expected to master the whole Bible this week. You are expected to start, and to keep going.

--- Desire, meditate, walk by the light
lit: Desire the sincere milk of the word | 1 Peter 2:2
lit: Meditate therein day and night | Joshua 1:8
lit: A lamp unto my feet | Psalm 119:105

--- A simple start
callout: **A simple start.** Read a little every day rather than a lot once a week. The Gospel of John is a good place to begin. It was written, in John’s own words, “that ye might believe” and, having believed, keep believing more deeply. And a caution: reading a devotional, a random verse of the day, or a one-line calendar entry is not a substitute for reading the Bible itself with a plan and a purpose, taking each passage in its context. Those tools are fine as seasoning; they were never meant to be the meal.
refs: John 20:31

=== VI. A life that pleases God
--- A life that pleases God
kicker: Session six
lead: God’s commandments were never meant to cage you. They were meant to protect you and, quite literally, to lengthen your days. The very first commandment with a promise attached tells us so directly.
scripture: Ephesians 6:1-3
lit: The commandment restated | Deuteronomy 5:16

--- The benefit is the point
p: Every command God gives is aimed at your good, not just your obedience. He isn’t fencing you in. He’s fencing out the things that would wreck your life. A few examples:
xr: Ephesians 4:25 | **Don’t lie**, because trust, once broken, is slow to rebuild; guard it instead.
xr: Ephesians 4:28 | **Don’t steal**, because a life built on taking never has anything left to give.
xr: Exodus 20:17 | **Don’t covet**, because a heart fixed on what it doesn’t have can never enjoy what it does.
xr: Titus 2:12 | **Be sober**, because a clear head is what lets you make good decisions in the moment they actually matter.
xr: 1 Corinthians 6:18 | **Don’t commit adultery or fornication.** It is a sin against your own body; it doesn’t just wound others, it wounds you.
take: God is not trying to make your life smaller. He’s showing you the path that actually works.

--- Your life is an open book test
p: People may never read the Bible you carry, but they are always reading you. That’s not a burden; it’s the privilege of being useful to God. You might be the only Bible they see.
image: lessons/bible/saved-christian-now-what-open-book.jpg | Life is an open book test: an open King James Bible on a table at sunrise

--- Known and read of all men
fig: Ye are our epistle, known and read of all men | 2 Corinthians 3:2-3
fig: All scripture is given by inspiration of God | 2 Timothy 3:16-17
fig: Quick, powerful, and sharper than a sword | Hebrews 4:12

--- The Book is open to you
p: But there’s a second sense in which your life is an open book: the Book itself is open to you. God didn’t leave you guessing about how to live. He wrote down the answer to nearly every question you’ll face: money, marriage, work, suffering, the future. Scripture isn’t a restriction on a good life; it’s the blueprint for one. The point of reading it isn’t to collect information. It’s to find out what God thinks, so you can walk the path that actually leads somewhere good.
scripture: Proverbs 3:5-6
refs: Psalm 119:105; Psalm 119:130

--- Expect trouble, and a way through it
p: Godly living was never promised to be easy. Christ said it plainly, and Paul confirmed it: living for Him will bring resistance, sometimes from the last people you’d expect.
fig: All that will live godly shall suffer persecution | 2 Timothy 3:12
fig: A way to escape temptation | 1 Corinthians 10:13
fig: A sword, not always peace | Matthew 10:34-36
fig: Division, even in a household | Luke 12:51-53

--- When family pushes back
callout: **Teaching point.** If a family member pushes back on your new faith, you are not failing. You are exactly where Jesus said some believers would be. Stay loving. Stay patient. Don’t force the issue, but don’t hide the change God has made in you either.

--- Love, in every direction
p: The proof that we belong to Christ is not how well we argue doctrine. It’s how we love the people around us.
group: The brethren | | 1 John 3:14; John 13:34-35
group: Your spouse | | Ephesians 5:25; Ephesians 5:33
group: Your parents, if you’re a child under their roof | | Ephesians 6:1
group: Those in authority over you | | Romans 13:1-7; Titus 3:1; 1 Peter 2:13-17
group: Your boss | | 1 Peter 2:18; Colossians 3:23-24
group: Your enemies | yes, even them | Matthew 5:44

--- When you sin (because you will)
p: Nobody in this course, including the one teaching it, has stopped sinning. Even Paul, decades into his walk with Christ, wrote honestly about losing the fight against his own flesh.
scripture: Romans 7:24
p: He doesn’t leave himself there. The very next breath answers the question, and the chapter that follows is the answer in full.

--- No condemnation
scripture: Romans 8:1-4
callout: **Teaching point.** Your flesh will keep sinning until the day you’re glorified. That’s not news, and it isn’t cause for despair. No temptation that comes at you is anything new; it’s common to everyone. But your standing before God isn’t decided by your flesh anymore. In Christ, you’ve been given a new man that isn’t a slave to sin, even while the old nature keeps fighting for control.

--- New every morning
fig: No temptation but such as is common to man | 1 Corinthians 10:13
fig: His mercies are new every morning | Lamentations 3:22-23
fig: Take up his cross daily | Luke 9:23
fig: Falls seven times, rises again | Proverbs 24:16
fig: As far as the east is from the west | Psalm 103:12
p: Following Christ was never a one-time decision that runs on autopilot. It’s a daily choice to lay the old self down again. Some days that means starting over more than once. That’s not failure; that’s the Christian life. Mercy meets you fresh every morning, and a fall doesn’t define you. Getting back up does.

--- Every sin, past, present and future, already covered
scripture: Romans 4:6-8
p: That’s not imputation one sin at a time. It’s a settled account. Every sin you had committed, are committing, and will ever commit was already covered when God declared you righteous in Christ. That truth isn’t a license to sin freely; it’s the very thing that makes honest confession possible instead of terrifying.
scripture: 1 John 1:9
p: Confession isn’t groveling to earn back what you never lost. It’s agreeing with God about what happened, so fellowship stays open and clean.

--- Walking in the Spirit
p: The real answer to sin was never willpower. It’s who you’re walking with.
fig: Walk in the Spirit | Galatians 5:16
fig: The fruit of the Spirit | Galatians 5:22-23
p: That fruit isn’t something you manufacture by trying harder. It grows out of walking close to Him. The more you do, the less the flesh has room to win.

--- Don’t do this alone
fig: Not forsaking the assembling of yourselves | Hebrews 10:24-25
fig: Charity unfeigned | 1 Peter 1:22
fig: Charity suffereth long | 1 Corinthians 13:4-7
p: Church is not an optional accessory to the Christian life. It’s where you’re loved, checked, fed, and kept from drifting. No believer in the New Testament grows alone.

--- Sober and vigilant
fig: Your adversary the devil | 1 Peter 5:8
fig: Let us watch and be sober | 1 Thessalonians 5:6
p: As the day of Christ’s return draws nearer, Scripture doesn’t tell us to relax. It tells us to stay alert, clear-headed, and ready.

=== VII. The hope of glory
--- The hope of glory
kicker: Session seven
lead: We close where every believer’s story is heading. Bible teachers disagree on the exact timing of certain end-times events, and this course won’t try to settle that argument for you. But on the big picture, God’s people have always agreed: Christ is coming back, the dead in Him will rise, we will be with Him forever, and every wrong will finally be made right.

--- The resurrection
scripture: 1 Corinthians 15:51-58

--- Caught up together
scripture: 1 Thessalonians 4:16-18
callout: **Teaching point.** Paul doesn’t end this passage with a debate. He ends it with “comfort one another with these words.” Whatever the exact order of events, this doctrine was given to comfort grieving believers, not to start arguments among them. Hold your view with conviction and hold your brother with grace.

--- Rule and reign
scripture: Revelation 20:4-6

--- New heaven, new earth
scripture: Revelation 21:1-4

--- We shall be like Him
fig: We shall be like him | 1 John 3:2
fig: New heavens and a new earth, wherein dwelleth righteousness | 2 Peter 3:13
fig: That blessed hope | Titus 2:13
take: This is your future. Live like it’s true, because it is.
